<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suintila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 621–631</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suintila was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> from 621 to 631. Around 624 he expelled the last Byzantine garrisons from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> praised him as the first king to rule the whole peninsula. He was overthrown in 631 by Sisenand with Frankish help, and the Fourth Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> later condemned him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suintila.jpg" alt="Suintila" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suintila_Tremis_Barbi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Suintila, also written Suinthila or Svinthila, was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reccared-i">Reccared I</a> and his wife Bado and a brother of the general Geila. He was born in about 588. When his older brother Liuva II was deposed in 603 he was spared by the nobility, possibly because of his age, for he was fifteen. Little is known of his life until about 620, when under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> he fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, who had re-established themselves on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> during the 550s. He was elected king the next year, after the deaths of Sisebut and Sisebut's son Reccared II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Suintila secured a peace unknown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> since the early 5th century, with no foreign troops on its soil for the next 87 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> had retaken Cartagena, Málaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Sagunto</a> and Assidonia from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and in 624 Suintila finished the work by seizing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, weakening Byzantine power in the former enclave. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> he was the first to rule all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Liuvigild</a> before him he tried to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> under his command, which led to the creation of a town named Ologicus, believed to be the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olite">Olite</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, though archaeologists have not confirmed this. Isidore praised his faith, prudence and generosity to the poor, calling him the father of the poor.</p><p>Others thought differently. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> reports that Suintila became hated for trying to make the kingdom hereditary, appointing his son Reccimer as co-regent in 626. In 631 the nobleman Sisenand rebelled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley after obtaining a promise of help from the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a>, whose generals Abundancius and Venerandus came to Saragossa and declared Sisenand king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the Fourth Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 633 the council legitimised Sisenand's seizure of power. Suintila was accused of various iniquities, forced to renounce power, excommunicated, stripped of his possessions and exiled with his family. His death is dated between 633 and 635. In 1858 a farmer's plough uncovered the Treasure of Guarrazar and Torredonjimeno, which included eleven votive crowns, three of them bearing names: Suintila, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth">Recceswinth</a> and Sónnica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suintila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leovigild retook inland areas, Sisebut captured Málaga around 615, and <b>Suintila</b> expelled the last imperial forces around 624.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzantine-spania">Byzantine Spania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=626" aria-label="Toledo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4451 737 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Suintila"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4901 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4901 1007h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=626">What was here in 626 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
